": "broaden"}, {"t": 2, "intent": "broaden"}, {"t": 2, "intent": "broaden"}, {"t": 2, "intent": "broaden"}, {"t": 2, "intent": "broaden"}], "considered_alternatives": ["B3", "B1.2", "B1.3"]}, "discriminator_hints": "[Knowledge Layer: coverage=80%, source=both]\n\nVascular Hypertensive Crisis vs Hypertensive Emergency with Secondary Complications:\n  Favours first:  anxiety, blood vessel damage in the eye, bulging neck veins, crackling sounds in lungs, heart murmurs\n  Favours second: facial droop, slurred speech, weakness in arms and/or legs\n\nVascular Hypertensive Crisis vs Acute Myocardial Infarction with Hypertensive Presentation:\n  Favours first:  altered mental status, blood vessel damage in the eye, bulging neck veins, crackling sounds in lungs, decreased urine output\n  Favours second: 2-3 toe syndactyly, abdominal distention, abnormal astrocyte morphology, abnormal brainstem mri signal intensity, abnormal epiphyseal ossification\n\nVascular Hypertensive Crisis vs Hypertensive Crisis in Dilated Cardiomyopathy:\n  Favours first:  altered mental status, anxiety, blood vessel damage in the eye, bulging neck veins, chest pain\n  Favours second: abnormal cardiac ventricular function, abnormal left ventricular function, abnormal lymphocyte physiology, abnormal myocardium morphology, atrial standstill\n\nHypertensive Emergency with Secondary Complications vs Acute Myocardial Infarction with Hypertensive Presentation:\n  Favours first:  altered mental status, decreased urine output, edema, facial droop, heart palpitations\n  Favours second: 2-3 toe syndactyly, abdominal distention, abnormal astrocyte morphology, abnormal brainstem mri signal intensity, abnormal epiphyseal ossification\n\nHypertensive Emergency with Secondary Complications vs Hypertensive Crisis in Dilated Cardiomyopathy:\n  Favours first:  altered mental status, chest pain, decreased urine output, dizziness, edema\n  Favours second: abnormal cardiac ventricular function, abnormal left ventricular function, abnormal lymphocyte physiology, abnormal myocardium morphology, atrial standstill\n\nAcute Myocardial Infarction with Hypertensive Presentation vs Hypertensive Crisis in Dilated Cardiomyopathy:\n  Favours first:  2-3 toe syndactyly, abdominal distention, abnormal astrocyte morphology, abnormal brainstem mri signal intensity, abnormal epiphyseal ossification\n  Favours second: abnormal cardiac ventricular function, abnormal left ventricular function, abnormal lymphocyte physiology, abnormal myocardium morphology, atrial standstill\n\nNOT typically seen in Hypertensive Crisis in Dilated Cardiomyopathy: elevated serum creatine kinase\n\nRelated to Acute Myocardial Infarction with Hypertensive Presentation: hemoglobinopathy\n\nRelated to Hypertensive Crisis in Dilated Cardiomyopathy: becker muscular dystrophy, duchenne muscular dystrophy, familial dilated cardiomyopathy\n\n[Indirect reasoning chains (PrimeKG 2-hop):]\n  A 55-year-old man presents to the emergency department with nausea and vomiting for the past week → abdominal distention → Acute Myocardial Infarction with Hypertensive Presentation\n  His wife recently returned from a cruise with symptoms of vomiting and diarrhea → abdominal distention → Acute Myocardial Infarction with Hypertensive Presentation"}
